<!-- Crash / power-loss behaviour (found and verified with chaos scenario 6).

     After a hard reset, parts written in the last ~30 s (kernel writeback
     window) can be left with empty files. ReplicatedMergeTree handles this:
     broken local parts are detached and fetched again from the other replicas.
     But if a table has more than max_suspicious_broken_parts (default 100)
     broken parts, the table refuses to load and the replica stays down until
     an operator intervenes. At ~5 inserts/s plus merges, one power cut breaks
     150-200 tiny parts, so the default count limit turns every crash into a
     manual recovery.

     - Raise the COUNT limit so the replica self-heals from its peers.
     - Keep the BYTES limit (max_suspicious_broken_parts_bytes, default 1 GiB):
       that is the guard that matters against real disasters such as a wrong or
       corrupted disk. The parts broken by a crash are ~0 bytes.
     - fsync inserted parts and their directories, so the replica that
       acknowledges an INSERT has it on disk. Parts fetched from peers and
       merged parts are not fsynced; a crash of those is repaired by re-fetch. -->
<clickhouse>
    <merge_tree>
        <max_suspicious_broken_parts>1000</max_suspicious_broken_parts>
        <fsync_after_insert>1</fsync_after_insert>
        <fsync_part_directory>1</fsync_part_directory>
    </merge_tree>
</clickhouse>
